# Backend method comments

When adding or changing C# in `api/` or `worker/`, document every method
with an XML summary that says what it does and why. Do this in the same
change as the method.

- Put the summary on the interface for a public service method. On the
  implementation, use `/// <inheritdoc />` and do not repeat the summary.
- Private methods, and public methods with no interface, get their own
  summary.
- Do not document constructors. A constructor that only stores its
  dependencies does not get a summary.
- Above each controller action, name the HTTP method and route, then the
  purpose. Example: `GET /api/accounts`.
- Do not add these comments to generated EF Core migration files.

```csharp
/// <summary>
/// Lists the signed-in household's accounts.
/// Archived accounts are omitted unless includeArchived is true.
/// </summary>
Task<IReadOnlyList<AccountDto>> GetAccountsAsync(...);

/// <inheritdoc />
public async Task<IReadOnlyList<AccountDto>> GetAccountsAsync(...)

/// <summary>
/// GET /api/accounts
/// Returns the household's accounts.
/// </summary>
[HttpGet]
public async Task<ActionResult<IReadOnlyList<AccountDto>>> GetAccounts(...)
```
